# Line endings, enforced in the repository rather than per machine.
#
# A shell script with CRLF does not run: the kernel reads `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r`
# and looks for an interpreter called `bash\r`, which does not exist. It happened
# on 11 November 2026 — an edit made on Windows rewrote
# docs/release/production-release.sh with CRLF, and the production release
# stopped at `bash\r: No such file or directory` with nothing else wrong.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is a per-clone setting, so it cannot prevent this for everyone.
# These rules travel with the repository and apply to every checkout.

# Default: normalise text on commit, leave the working tree to the platform.
* text=auto

# Anything executed by a shell or a kernel shebang must be LF in the working
# tree too, whatever the platform — these are run on Linux hosts and in
# containers regardless of where they were edited.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.cjs text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.dockerfile text eol=lf
.husky/* text eol=lf

# Binary: never touched.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf binary
*.xlsx binary
*.docx binary
*.zip binary
*.enc binary
